  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Glucose</mi><munderover><mo>→</mo><mo>∆</mo><msub><mi>NaHCO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></munderover><mi>no</mi><mo> </mo><mi>reaction</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Glucose</mi><munderover><mo>→</mo><mo>∆</mo><msub><mi>HNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></munderover><mi>saccharic</mi><mo> </mo><mi>acid</mi></math></p><p>Glucose, on oxidation with nitric acid, gives saccharic acid.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Glucose</mi><munderover><mo>→</mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>HI</mi></munderover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>hexane</mi></math></p><p>Glucose, on prolonged heating with hot HI, gives n-Hexane.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Glucose</mi><munderover><mo>→</mo><mo>∆</mo><msub><mi>Br</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></munderover><mi>Gluconic</mi><mo> </mo><mi>acid</mi></math></p><p>Glucose reacts with bromine water to form gluconic acid.</p><p> Bromine water is a mild oxidising agent which selectively oxidizes aldehyde to carboxylic acid only. It doesn’t oxidise alcohol or ketone.</p><p>Hence option B is the answer.</p>"
